! nalu-engine — Invariant

!! Identity

`nalu-engine` is a TW5 `module-type: startup` module written in TS, compiled into the
plugin bundle, and evaluated inside the wiki at boot. It installs the `$tw.lares.*` API
that the TS-side `IslandAdaptor` calls across the shore.

The wiki IS the reactive engine (yin-collapse law, lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/nalu).
This module lands the nalu wave in the wiki. It answers HOW one change lands; the wiki shore answers
WHEN (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge`).

!! $tw.lares.* surface

| Method | Caller | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `applyNalu(change)` | the wiki shore (`IslandAdaptor`), once per landed change | Land ONE change synchronously: a tombstone or an absent record deletes the title; any other record adds the tiddler the nalu form builds from the envelope |
| `applyNaluCrest(run)` | the wiki shore (`WaveLanding`), once per slice | Land ONE crest: every apply `run` makes lands in one synchronous stretch, the apply flag raised across the whole crest, so the wiki tells its observers once |
| `isApplyingNalu()` | a synchronous caller | True while an `applyNalu` call or a crest runs |

`$tw.lares.enqueueNalu` belongs to the wiki shore. The shore installs its queue door there and removes
it at teardown; this module never holds one.

!! The nalu form ~ one form above and below

`naluFieldsOf(change)` (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/nalu-form.ts`, a leaf the packed module bundles by
its relative path) names the one shape a change takes as a wiki tiddler: the record's fields, `title`
restored from the envelope, the draft-base fields left on the store record, and `$origin-bag` stamped
from the envelope's bag. Below, every wave lands through it (`applyNalu`, `toWikiFields`). Above, wave
zero hands the seed to `$tw.preloadTiddlers` through it before TiddlyWiki starts, and the glass mirror
boots its store through it. The shore's landing compares the same form with what stands
(`sameWithStamp`), so a preloaded title and the record a later landing of the same change would write
carry the same fields byte for byte, and the landing writes nothing. The sameness holds by
construction, one function above and below: a pattern integrity of the nalu
(<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/nalu#/the-wave-shape`>>).

!! Position in the stack

```
TS shore (irreducible — Automerge, network, crypto, storage)
  AutomergeDocStore → MemeProvider → projection bus → IslandAdaptor.onUriChanged
  the landing queue (WaveLanding) → prepares a slice, then opens one crest
                              → $tw.lares.applyNaluCrest(run)
                                  → $tw.lares.applyNalu(change) × N
  wave zero (island-seed) → naluFieldsOf(change) × N → $tw.preloadTiddlers
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TW5 startup module (this spec)
  applyNaluCrest(run)                     ← one synchronous stretch, the flag raised throughout
  applyNalu(change)                       ← wiki.addTiddler / wiki.deleteTiddler
  _applying flag                          ← read through isApplyingNalu()
```

!! Invariants

''N-1 One applier per wiki.''
The module holds one wiki and one `_applying` flag at module level. Every inbound change enters the
shore's queue, whatever its origin kind; the shore calls `applyNalu` when the value it resolved
differs from the value the wiki holds, and every such write passes through this one applier.

''N-2 One change, one synchronous stretch; one crest, one dispatch.''
`applyNalu` lands exactly one change and returns. `applyNaluCrest(run)` lands a crest: `run` makes its
applies in one synchronous stretch and returns. The engine holds no queue, no timer and no frame,
and it wraps nothing in a transaction: TW5 gathers changed titles until its own scheduled
`nextTick` dispatch, so a crest reaches the widgets as ONE dispatch. The engine names that law; the
shore keeps its per-change ledger and hand bookkeeping inside `run`. A `run` that awaits ends the
crest at its first await, and the applies after it scatter across dispatches.

''N-3 The queue belongs to the shore, one shore per engine.''
The wiki shore owns the ordered queue, its slices, the host-task pacing between them and the seed
checkpoint. It installs its door only where `applyNalu` already stands (else
`LandingDoorUnavailableError`), and a second shore on an engine whose door stands occupied refuses by
`LandingDoorOccupiedError`. An engine whose applier carries no crest door lands the shore's slice
bare, in the same single tick.

''N-4 The apply flag covers the call or the crest, never the dispatch.''
`_applying` reads `true` for the length of one `applyNalu` call or one crest and returns to the value
it found in a `finally`, so an apply that begins inside another leaves the outer one raised. TW5
dispatches `change` on `nextTick`, after the flag lowered, so a `change` listener never reads it
raised. The wiki shore therefore tells a wave from a hand by WHAT STANDS: its landed ledger holds the
value each wave left under each title, and a notification that repeats that value reads as foam
([[outbound-bridge|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge]] #/the-shore-law).

''N-5 Tiddler fields = the nalu form.''
For a non-tombstone change, the engine builds the wiki fields through `naluFieldsOf`: the record's
fields with the draft base left home, `title` restored from `change.title`, and `$origin-bag` stamped
from `change.bag` when the envelope names one. `bag` stays the author's own field and rides through
untouched. For a tombstone (`record === null` or `record.meta?.deleted === true`), the engine
calls `wiki.deleteTiddler(change.title)`.

''N-6 No wiki, no apply.''
An `applyNalu` call or a crest with no wiki under it throws `NaluWikiUnavailableError`, which names
the title.

''N-7 Idempotent startup.''
`startup()` reads `$tw.lares ?? {}`, installs the three functions, writes back. Safe to
run more than once in tests; subsequent invocations overwrite with identical refs.

!! The seed ~ wave zero

The seed lands as the first nalu, delivered before TiddlyWiki starts. The kernel lays the composition
before the engine boots (`layIslandComposition`), reads it once (`readIslandSeed`: one `resolveLayered`
per title, topmost wins, a tombstone included, the boot set's own titles left out), and hands each
record in the nalu form to the engine as a preload. TiddlyWiki adds the preloads while its change queue
still sleeps, so wave zero raises no `change` dispatch, and it defines their modules, unpacks their
plugins and runs their startup actions as it does any store's. The shore then mounts
(`mountIslandShore`) and drives `emitInitialReplay()` per bag as before: each preloaded title compares
the same form with what stands and writes nothing, while the ledger, the landed heads, the draft book
and the kahua shadow stamps fill through the one road. Every change the composition told while the
engine booted lands afresh, so a record that left every layer during the boot leaves the wiki too.
The shore's seed checkpoint resolves once its queue and its tracked work settle, and the kernel awaits
it before it arms the island's behavior. Later waves carry what changed after the seed.

<<~ ahu slot=`#/gate-families-shore`>>

!! The gate-families shore ~ runtime ≠ physics

ONE shore queue holds the changes of every bag (N-3). Upstream,
`projection-nalu` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/projection-nalu`) stands a `KeyedCoalesceGate`
''per bag''. Both stand; they sit at OPPOSITE ENDS of the one shape `SOURCE → gate → SINK`, never
the same layer.

<<~ scale name=`shore` ranks=`UPSTREAM/provider-coalesce-per-key -> DOWNSTREAM/shore-landing-queue-per-slice -> ENGINE/apply-crest`>>

* ''UPSTREAM — the provider gates (physics).'' `MemeProvider` (the TS-side, before the
  wiki shore) draws the coalesce gates: a per-URI debounce collapses the replay patches a fresh
  vessel-connect fires for one URI down to ONE latest-state change, and the `_changesetGate`
  (`meme-provider.ts`) coalesces a storm of large Automerge transactions to ONE
  `onChangeset(union)` per bag. This names the ''coalesce'' family — intermediates lawfully DROP,
  newest-per-key wins. The drop happens HERE, before a change ever reaches the landing queue.
* ''DOWNSTREAM — the landing queue (runtime).'' Each surviving change reaches
  the island adaptor and joins the shore's ONE queue in arrival order, a changeset as one family. The
  shore processes whole families, up to 200 queued changes per slice, and yields to the host between slices; each
  landing resolves its title through the composition, and the shore calls `applyNalu` when that
  value differs from the value the wiki holds. A landing that fails twice stays owed for a later
  wave; a standing shore coalesces NOTHING further.

So "one queue, all bags" governs the DELIVERY runtime — the shore refuses to re-shard its queue per
bag. The per-key coalesce gates do not contradict it, because they live UPSTREAM, shaping the stream
BEFORE it reaches the queue. ''The shore and the engine carry the runtime; the gate-families carry the''
''physics it enacts'' (`role = capability ≠ platform`). The three compose end-to-end:
coalesce-per-key (drop intermediates) → one queue (preserve order) → one crest per slice (land, one dispatch). WITNESSED: the
applier (`modules/nalu-engine.ts`) + the queue (`wave-landing.ts`, `island-adaptor.ts`) + the provider
gates (`mesh/meme-provider.ts`, `mesh/projection-nalu.ts`).

